From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: arguments in registers for a call this unit can see

Every argument on this target goes on the stack: the caller pushes it,
the callee reads it back through an index, and the caller takes it off
again.  For a small function that is most of what the call costs, and
the machine has two registers -- R0 and R1 -- that a call is already
free to destroy.

A register convention can only be used where both sides of the call are
certain to agree about it.  Without an attribute to declare one, that
means a function this unit can see every call to: the call names a
declaration, the declaration is local to the translation unit and its
address is never taken, so no indirect call can reach it.  That is what
cgraph's "local" and "can_change_signature" say, and it is what the x86
port uses to the same end.  The answer is taken from the callee, not the
caller, so that both sides compute it the same way; and because the
flag is only there once the IPA passes have run, -O0 keeps the stack
convention, which also keeps a program easy to follow in a debugger.

The window is the two call-clobbered registers and nothing more.  A
wider one would mean telling the generic code, per call, that this call
destroys registers the standard convention preserves, and this port has
none of that machinery.  Within the window: integers and pointers only,
since a floating value wants the registers the callee saves and an
aggregate is passed as the block of memory it is; a value of two words
has to start on an even register, which is all pdp11_hard_regno_mode_ok
allows, and has to fit whole, because an argument is never split
between a register and the stack; and once one argument has gone to the
stack the rest follow it, so what is left on the stack is the picture
the standard convention draws, minus a prefix.

Nothing else moves.  A library call, a call through a pointer, a call to
anything outside the unit, a variable argument list -- all keep the
convention they had, so objects built earlier, the hand-written
assembly in libgcc and modules from a foreign assembler go on working
unchanged.  -mno-reg-args turns the whole thing off.

Twenty thousand calls in a loop, frames at fifty to the second:

                     stack   registers
  f(int, int)          116          94
  f(int, int, int)     123         105
  f(long)              113          96
  f(long, int)         127         133

The last line is the one case where it costs: the pushed int needs a
scratch register while the pair is held, the allocator takes R0 for it,
and the accumulator that would have stayed in the return pair is
spilled instead.  examples/spigot, which is division in a loop and
calls nothing local in it, goes 13893 -> 13875 frames and 12736 ->
12720 bytes of text.

Tested with three new target tests; with a sample of 120
gcc.c-torture/execute tests -- the va-arg and structure-passing ones,
the nested-function ones, and the block beginning with 2 -- run on the
machine both ways, which give the same results in both (one failure,
nestfunc-3, is the trampolines, and fails without this too); and with
libgcc, newlib and the repository's own tests and examples rebuilt and
run.

---
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-protos.h             |    1 
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc                   |   99 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h                    |   36 +++++++---
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.opt                  |    4 +
 gcc/doc/invoke.texi                         |   13 +++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-1.c |   22 ++++++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-2.c |   15 ++++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-3.c |   25 +++++++
 8 files changed, 202 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-protos.h b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-protos.h
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-protos.h	2026-10-12 20:07:20
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11-protos.h	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -48,6 +48,7 @@
 
 #endif /* RTX_CODE */
 
+extern int pdp11_arg_regs (const_tree, const_tree);
 extern void output_ascii (FILE *, const char *, int);
 extern void pdp11_asm_output_var (FILE *, const char *, int, int, bool);
 extern void pdp11_expand_prologue (void);
diff -urN a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc	2026-10-12 20:07:20
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include "regs.h"
 #include "emit-rtl.h"
 #include "recog.h"
+#include "cgraph.h"
 #include "conditions.h"
 #include "output.h"
 #include "stor-layout.h"
@@ -2362,12 +2363,100 @@
   emit_move_insn (mem, fnaddr);
 }
 
+/* How many words of a call's arguments may be handed over in
+   registers: PDP11_NARGREGS, or none at all.
+
+   The standard convention of this machine is that arguments go on the
+   stack, and that is what every object this compiler did not just
+   produce expects -- the library, the assembly a program brings with
+   it, the modules a foreign assembler made.  A register convention can
+   only be used where both sides of the call are known to be compiled
+   the same way, and without a declared attribute to say so, that means
+   a function this unit can see every call to: FNDECL names it, its
+   address is never taken, and nothing outside can reach it.  The
+   cgraph flags below say exactly that, and they are what the x86 port
+   uses for the same purpose.
+
+   The answer has to come out the same when the caller is expanded and
+   when the callee is, which is why it is taken from the callee: the
+   optimisation level is read off the callee's own decl, since an
+   attribute can make the two differ.  */
+
+int
+pdp11_arg_regs (const_tree fntype, const_tree fndecl)
+{
+  cgraph_node *node;
+
+  if (fndecl == NULL_TREE || TREE_CODE (fndecl) != FUNCTION_DECL)
+    return 0;
+
+  /* A variable argument list is walked as one block of stack, so the
+     named arguments in front of it cannot start in registers.  */
+  if (fntype != NULL_TREE && stdarg_p (fntype))
+    return 0;
+
+  node = cgraph_node::get (fndecl);
+  if (node != NULL)
+    node = node->function_symbol ();
+  if (node == NULL || !node->local || !node->can_change_signature)
+    return 0;
+
+  /* The optimisation level is read off the callee's own decl, because an
+     attribute can make the caller's differ and the two sides have to
+     reach the same answer.  -mreg-args needs no such care: this port has
+     no per-function target options for a pragma to change.  */
+  if (!TARGET_REG_ARGS || !opt_for_fn (node->decl, optimize))
+    return 0;
+
+  return PDP11_NARGREGS;
+}
+
+/* Where ARG goes: its size in words if it is to travel in registers,
+   with *REGNO (when REGNO is given) set to the first of them, and zero
+   if it belongs in memory.  */
+
+static int
+pdp11_arg_in_regs (CUMULATIVE_ARGS *cum, const function_arg_info &arg,
+		   unsigned int *regno)
+{
+  int words, first;
+
+  if (cum->regs_left == 0 || arg.end_marker_p ())
+    return 0;
+
+  /* Integers and pointers only.  A floating value would want the
+     floating registers, which the callee saves, and an aggregate is
+     passed as the block of memory it is.  */
+  if (!SCALAR_INT_MODE_P (arg.mode) || arg.aggregate_type_p ())
+    return 0;
+
+  words = CEIL (arg.promoted_size_in_bytes ().to_constant (), UNITS_PER_WORD);
+  first = PDP11_NARGREGS - cum->regs_left;
+
+  /* Two words or more have to start on an even register, which is what
+     pdp11_hard_regno_mode_ok allows, and have to fit whole: this
+     convention never splits an argument between a register and the
+     stack.  */
+  if (words > cum->regs_left || (words > 1 && (first & 1) != 0))
+    return 0;
+
+  if (regno != NULL)
+    *regno = first;
+  return words;
+}
+
 /* Worker function for TARGET_FUNCTION_ARG.  */
 
 static rtx
-pdp11_function_arg (cumulative_args_t, const function_arg_info &)
+pdp11_function_arg (cumulative_args_t cum_v, const function_arg_info &arg)
 {
-  return NULL_RTX;
+  CUMULATIVE_ARGS *cum = get_cumulative_args (cum_v);
+  unsigned int regno;
+
+  if (pdp11_arg_in_regs (cum, arg, &regno) == 0)
+    return NULL_RTX;
+
+  return gen_rtx_REG (arg.mode, regno);
 }
 
 /* Worker function for TARGET_FUNCTION_ARG_ADVANCE.
@@ -2379,8 +2468,12 @@
 			    const function_arg_info &arg)
 {
   CUMULATIVE_ARGS *cum = get_cumulative_args (cum_v);
+  int words = pdp11_arg_in_regs (cum, arg, NULL);
 
-  *cum += arg.promoted_size_in_bytes ();
+  /* Once one argument has gone to the stack the rest follow it, so
+     that what is left on the stack is the picture the standard
+     convention draws, minus a prefix.  */
+  cum->regs_left = (words == 0) ? 0 : cum->regs_left - words;
 }
 
 /* Make sure everything's fine if we *don't* have an FPU.
diff -urN a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h	2026-10-12 20:07:20
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -371,9 +371,19 @@
  (FLOAT_MODE_P (MODE) ? AC0_REGNUM : RETVAL_REGNUM)
 
 /* 1 if N is a possible register number for function argument passing.
-   - not used on pdp */
+   The two call-clobbered registers, and only where pdp11_arg_regs lets
+   the register convention be used at all.  */
 
-#define FUNCTION_ARG_REGNO_P(N) 0
+#define FUNCTION_ARG_REGNO_P(N) ((N) == 0 || (N) == 1)
+
+/* How many words of arguments that convention may carry, and so how
+   many registers starting at R0 it uses.  The pair is R0 and R1
+   because those are the two a caller does not expect to get back (see
+   CALL_USED_REGISTERS); a wider window would mean telling the generic
+   code, per call, that a call clobbers more than the standard
+   convention clobbers, and nothing here says that yet.  */
+
+#define PDP11_NARGREGS 2
 
 /* Define a data type for recording info about an argument list
    during the scan of that argument list.  This data type should
@@ -381,20 +391,28 @@
    and about the args processed so far, enough to enable macros
    such as FUNCTION_ARG to determine where the next arg should go.
 
-*/
+   Here that is how many of the argument registers are still free: the
+   stack offsets are the generic code's own business, and nothing in
+   this port reads them back.  */
 
-#define CUMULATIVE_ARGS int
+struct pdp11_args
+{
+  int regs_left;
+};
 
+#define CUMULATIVE_ARGS struct pdp11_args
+
 /* Initialize a variable CUM of type CUMULATIVE_ARGS
    for a call to a function whose data type is FNTYPE.
    For a library call, FNTYPE is 0.
 
-   ...., the offset normally starts at 0, but starts at 1 word
-   when the function gets a structure-value-address as an
-   invisible first argument.  */
+   FNDECL is the function being called, or null when the target of the
+   call is not a declaration this unit can see -- a library call, or a
+   call through a pointer.  Whether an argument may travel in a
+   register is decided from it, once per call, by pdp11_arg_regs.  */
 
-#define INIT_CUMULATIVE_ARGS(CUM, FNTYPE, LIBNAME, INDIRECT, N_NAMED_ARGS) \
- ((CUM) = 0)
+#define INIT_CUMULATIVE_ARGS(CUM, FNTYPE, LIBNAME, FNDECL, N_NAMED_ARGS) \
+ ((CUM).regs_left = pdp11_arg_regs ((FNTYPE), (FNDECL)))
 
 /* Output assembler code to FILE to increment profiler label # LABELNO
    for profiling a function entry.  */
diff -urN a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.opt b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.opt
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.opt	2026-10-12 20:07:20
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.opt	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -85,3 +85,7 @@
 mlra
 Target Mask(LRA)
 Use LRA register allocator.
+
+mreg-args
+Target Var(TARGET_REG_ARGS) Init(1)
+Pass the first arguments of a call in registers where both sides of the call are compiled together.
diff -urN a/gcc/doc/invoke.texi b/gcc/doc/invoke.texi
--- a/gcc/doc/invoke.texi	2026-10-12 20:07:20
+++ b/gcc/doc/invoke.texi	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -1251,7 +1251,8 @@
 @gccoptlist{-mfpu  -msoft-float  -mfis  -mno-fis  -mac0  -mno-ac0
 -m40  -m45  -m10
 -mint32  -mno-int16  -mint16  -mno-int32
--msplit  -munix-asm  -mdec-asm  -mgnu-asm  -mlra}
+-msplit  -munix-asm  -mdec-asm  -mgnu-asm  -mlra
+-mreg-args  -mno-reg-args}
 
 @emph{PowerPC Options}
 See RS/6000 and PowerPC Options.
@@ -30944,6 +30945,16 @@
 @item -mlra
 Use the new LRA register allocator.  By default, the old ``reload''
 allocator is used.
+
+@opindex mreg-args
+@opindex mno-reg-args
+@item -mreg-args
+Pass the first two words of a call's arguments in R0 and R1 where the
+called function is one the compiler can see every call to: a function
+local to the translation unit whose address is never taken.  Everything
+else keeps the standard convention, in which all arguments are pushed on
+the stack.  This is the default; @option{-mno-reg-args} passes every
+argument on the stack.
 @end table
 
 @node PowerPC Options
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-1.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-1.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-1.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-1.c	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* A function this unit can see every call to takes its first two words
+   of arguments in R0 and R1, so the body works on the registers and
+   reads nothing off the stack.  */
+
+static int __attribute__((noinline,noclone)) loc (int a, int b)
+{
+  return a - b;
+}
+
+extern int ext (int a, int b);
+
+int use (int x, int y)
+{
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "sub\tr1,r0" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "sub\t0?4\\(sp\\),r0" } } */
+  /* The external one cannot be seen, so it keeps the stack
+     convention.  */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "jsr\tpc,_?ext" } } */
+  return loc (x, y) + ext (x, y);
+}
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-2.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-2.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-2.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-2.c	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2 -mno-reg-args" } */
+/* -mno-reg-args puts every argument back on the stack.  */
+
+static int __attribute__((noinline,noclone)) loc (int a, int b)
+{
+  return a - b;
+}
+
+int use (int x, int y)
+{
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "sub\t0?4\\(sp\\),r0" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "sub\tr1,r0" } } */
+  return loc (x, y);
+}
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-3.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-3.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-3.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-3.c	2026-10-12 20:07:20
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* Two kinds of local function that still take their arguments on the
+   stack: one whose address is taken, because the indirect call cannot
+   know anything else, and one with a variable argument list, which is
+   walked as one block of stack.  */
+
+static int __attribute__((noinline,noclone)) taken (int a, int b)
+{
+  return a - b;
+}
+
+static int __attribute__((noinline,noclone)) va (int n, ...)
+{
+  return n;
+}
+
+int (*volatile pf) (int, int) = taken;
+
+int use (int x, int y)
+{
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "sub\t0?4\\(sp\\),r0" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "sub\tr1,r0" } } */
+  return taken (x, y) + pf (x, y) + va (x, y);
+}
